# SpecimenFindingsTeam rules — `significant_findings`

This section is the **surgical-pathology narrative**. Emit the object with
`count_of_specimen_findings` (= length of `specimen_findings`),
`llm_confidence_score`, and `specimen_findings` (one object per specimen).
Your **exclusive source** is the part of the report explicitly labelled "Final
Diagnosis", "Pathologic Diagnosis", "Diagnosis", "Synoptic Report",
"Gross Description", "Microscopic Description", "Opinion", or "Impression".

If this report has NO surgical-pathology content (e.g. a molecular/PCR assay,
a blood test) emit an **empty** `specimen_findings: []` with
`count_of_specimen_findings: 0`. Do not invent specimens.

## 1. Specimen identification — one entry per specimen (or one aggregated)

- If the report has **multiple labelled specimens** (A, B, C / 1, 2, 3), create
  one `specimen_findings` entry per specimen and map each finding to the right
  one by its identifier.
- If specimens exist but the findings are **aggregated and cannot be separated**,
  create **one** entry whose `specimen[]` lists all the specimens.
- For each specimen in `specimen[]`: `specimen_id` ("A"), `tissue_type`
  ("Breast", "Lymph Node"), `description`, `tissue_site` ("RIGHT BREAST
  RETROAREOLAR (MRI-GUIDED CORE BIOPSY)"), `laterality` (Right/Left/Bilateral).
- **`laterality` is per-specimen — derive it from THIS specimen's own
  site/description, never copy it from a sibling specimen.** Each specimen
  carries its own side: a right-breast biopsy is `Right`, a left-axilla node is
  `Left`. If a specimen's own text states no side, emit `null` rather than
  inheriting another specimen's laterality.

## 2. Per-specimen detail (map to the CORRECT specimen)

- `procedure_details`: `procedure` ("Core Biopsy", "Excisional Biopsy",
  "Resection"), `specimen_count` (integer or null).
- `gross_description.text`: the entire gross description for THIS specimen. If
  the gross section is labelled per-specimen (A) … B) …), map A's text to
  specimen A.
- `microscopic_description.text`: the microscopic / diagnosis text for THIS
  specimen (may live under Final Diagnosis / Diagnosis / Opinion / Impression).
- `histologic_findings[]`: one object per conclusive histologic diagnosis —
  `finding` ("Invasive Ductal Carcinoma"), `interpretation` (or null).
- `ancillary_studies_findings[]`: high-level summary of ancillary tests (IHC,
  FISH, NGS) — `ancillary_finding`, `interpretation`. NOTE: detailed biomarker
  results still belong in the biomarker umbrellas; here keep it summary-level.
- `morphology_findings`: `nuclear_grade`, `growth_pattern`, `size`, `necrosis`,
  `calcifications`, `tumor_extent` (each VERBATIM or null).

## 3. pTNM staging — capture the version, it is CRITICAL

- `pTNM_staging_details.pTNM_stage`: the full alphanumeric AJCC string (T, N, M
  components — assemble if split across lines, e.g. "pT2 pN1a M0").
- `pTNM_staging_details.staging_system_version`: **CRITICAL** — scan the text
  around the TNM scores for the staging authority + edition (e.g. "AJCC 8th
  Edition", "AJCC Cancer Staging Manual, 8th ed."). Do not leave this null if
  the report states an edition anywhere near the staging.
- **Case-level staging applies to every specimen it describes.** A synoptic
  pTNM stage is usually stated once for the whole case but spans multiple
  specimens — the `pT` component describes the tumor specimen, the `pN`
  component the nodal specimen. Record the same `pTNM_stage` +
  `staging_system_version` on each specimen the stage pertains to (e.g. the
  primary-tumor specimen AND the regional-node specimen), not only on the first.

## 4. Lymph nodes

- `lymph_node_details`: `lymph_node_status` (Positive/Negative/Tumor present/
  Benign), `number_of_lymph_nodes_examined` (integer), `number_of_lymph_nodes_positive`
  (integer — count micrometastases / ITCs if the report counts them as positive),
  `text` (the nodal-findings block: extranodal extension, deposit size, sentinel
  vs non-sentinel). Look in "Synoptic Report" / "Regional Lymph Nodes".

## 5. page_number + llm_confidence_score

Per-object `page_number` is DERIVED (where that content sits). Emit a top-level
`llm_confidence_score` in [0.0, 1.0] for the section.

